<h2>When you need a root canal</h2>
<p>Root canal therapy is needed when the pulp (the soft tissue inside a tooth that contains nerves and blood vessels) becomes infected or severely inflamed — usually from deep decay, a crack, or trauma. Common symptoms include lingering sensitivity to hot or cold, pain when biting, a pimple on the gum, or a tooth that throbs at night.</p>

<h2>What the treatment involves</h2>
<ol class="checklist" style="list-style:decimal;padding-left:28px">
  <li>The tooth is thoroughly numbed.</li>
  <li>A small opening is made to access the inside of the tooth.</li>
  <li>The infected pulp tissue is cleaned out and the canals disinfected.</li>
  <li>The canals are sealed with a flexible filling material.</li>
  <li>A temporary filling closes the tooth until a permanent crown is placed (usually within a few weeks).</li>
</ol>

<h2>The alternative is losing the tooth</h2>
<p>If a tooth is causing pain because of infection, the only alternatives to a root canal are extraction (and then a replacement, usually an implant or a bridge) or living with the infection &mdash; which is never a good idea. Saving your natural tooth is almost always the best option, and that's what root canal therapy is for.</p>
